# Skill: feature-developer

Read and follow `skills/feature-builder/feature-developer/SKILL.md`. Fill `skills/feature-builder/feature-developer/assets/what-was-implemented.md` only.

Announce: `Using skill: feature-developer`.

## Job

Build what `what-to-build.md` specifies. Nothing else.

## Must (order)

1. Create and check out a feature branch from the default branch. Stop and ask if the tree is dirty or the branch already exists. Use the `cursor/` prefix unless this repo's branches show another.
2. Add only the structure this repo already uses.
3. Implement in-scope behavior. Obey walls. Match local style. Run existing tests if a command exists; do not invent a runner. Record failures that were already red as pre-existing.
4. Write `<root>/<name>/what-was-implemented.md` (status, branch, base, commit, what, why, where, how to try). `<root>` is the docs **folder** named on the Feature-folder write-ups line in `.cursor/rules/agent-engineer-skills.mdc`, else `agent-engineer-skills`.
5. Commit on the feature branch. Do not push.

Windows PowerShell 5.1 has no `&&` operator; run git commands one per call there.

If `what-to-build.md` is missing, use `concept.md` if present. If still fuzzy, stop and use `feature-specifier`. If the request is a defect without a locked analysis, stop and use `feature-bug-analyst`. If the request is a messy-module cleanup with no specification, stop and use `feature-refactorer`. If a new landmine appears, stop and ask.

Stop after the write-up. Do not start `feature-code-reviewer` unless the operator named that skill.

When Origin is `feature-bug-analyst`, write the proposed failing test first, confirm it fails, then apply the minimal fix.

## Must not

- Planning packs, CI, threat-model essays
- New CI, new test framework, extra product
- Silent scope
- Push, commit to the default branch, amend, or rebase
- Icons or emojis in the write-up
